---
source: crates/dlin/tests/integration/cli.rs
expression: long_help(args)
---
Column-level lineage and impact analysis.

Unlike `dlin graph` (model-level, bidirectional, with -u/-d depth control),
column analysis is split by direction — each subcommand covers one direction:

  upstream    — traces where each output column's data came from
  downstream  — finds which models/columns are affected by a column change

There are no -u/-d depth flags; the full chain is always traversed in both cases.

Unlike `dlin graph`/`dlin impact`, there is no --source flag — manifest.json is the only
data source for column-level analysis.

Both subcommands require manifest.json with compiled SQL (run `dbt compile` first).
Use `dlin check-manifest` to verify freshness before querying.

Limitations:
  - Value dependencies only: columns used only in filters and join
    conditions (row dependencies) are not collected.
  - Unknown columns behind SELECT *, unsupported SQL and ambiguous names
    can make upstream lineage partial and downstream exploration incomplete.
  - A STRUCT field can be traced to the inputs of the whole STRUCT, so a
    complete result can include the inputs of sibling fields. This happens
    across dbt model boundaries, and through CAST, CASE, set operations,
    array subscripts or UNNEST of an array built elsewhere.
  - A YAML column list used as a schema is treated as exhaustive. Results
    that rely on it cite it in evidence[] in JSON and show an "assumes"
    note in plain, Mermaid and DOT output.

Usage: dlin column [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  upstream    Compute column-level lineage for a model (traces upstream sources)
  downstream  Analyze downstream column-level impact of changing a column
  help        Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
      --error-format <ERROR_FORMAT>
          Error/warning output format on stderr: text (default) or json.
          
          When json, each diagnostic is one JSON object on its own line. Every
          object has {"level":"error"|"warning"|"info","what":"...","why":...,"hint":...},
          where why and hint are either strings or null. Typed diagnostics add a
          "code" and fields of their own; for example dependency_analysis_summary
          adds counts by reason type and namespace. Read code and the typed fields,
          not the text of what, why and hint.

          Possible values:
          - text: Human-readable error messages (default)
          - json: Structured JSON on stderr: {"level":"...","what":"...","why":...,"hint":...}
          
          [env: DLIN_ERROR_FORMAT=]
          [default: text]

      --verbose
          Show the details of the model dependency analysis on stderr.
          
          By default, a command that builds the lineage graph prints at most one
          configuration warning and one dependency_analysis_summary diagnostic, which
          counts the reasons of the whole project by type and namespace. With
          --verbose it also prints one diagnostic per reason (with the node and the
          call site) and per skipped input, and the info diagnostics for default
          paths that do not exist. It changes no analysis. --quiet takes precedence.

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

Examples:
  dlin column upstream orders                             # upstream: where do columns come from?
  dlin column downstream stg_orders --column order_id     # downstream: what depends on this column?
